The <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149715">iOS 27.2.1</a>, iPadOS 27.2.1, and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149718">macOS 27.2.1</a> updates Apple released today fix a handful of security vulnerabilities. With the security fixes implemented, it's a good idea to update as soon as you can.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/05/bug-security-vulnerability-issue-fix-larry.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-896108" /><br/>
A bug with iMessage could let a remote attacker join a group chat to receive its messages, and another could let users see messages sent before they joined a group chat.<br/>
<br/>
Most of the vulnerabilities involved <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/airplay/">AirPlay</a> and Bluetooth. A malicious Bluetooth device could pair without user interaction, and another bug could let a nearby attacker corrupt process memory. Several bugs could allow an attacker to bypass the rate limit on &zwnj;AirPlay&zwnj; pairing code attempts.<br/>
<br/>
Apple credits itself on four of the vulnerabilities, so they were found internally.<br/>
<br/>
With the macOS update, Apple also <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/09/macos-27-2-1-emudhra-fix/">addressed the eMudhra certificate vulnerability</a>, adding protections for command-line tools.<br/>
<br/>
